@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Rollback Strategies in Azure DevOps";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">Rollback strategies in Azure DevOps help organizations **mitigate deployment failures** and ensure application stability by reverting to a previous working state.</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What Are Rollback Strategies?</h2>
        <p>Rollback strategies provide a way to **undo faulty deployments** and restore a previous stable version of an application.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Helps **minimize downtime** and ensure application reliability.</li>
            <li>Supports **automated and manual rollbacks**.</li>
            <li>Prevents failed deployments from affecting end users.</li>
            <li>Can be configured with **Azure DevOps Pipelines**.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/rollback-strategy.png" alt="Rollback Strategies in Azure DevOps" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Rollback Strategies in Azure DevOps Pipelines</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Types of Rollback Strategies</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>**Manual Rollback** – Manually redeploy a previous stable build if issues occur.</li>
                <li>**Automated Rollback** – Configure pipeline triggers to detect failures and revert automatically.</li>
                <li>**Blue-Green Deployment** – Run two environments (one live, one idle) and switch traffic if a failure occurs.</li>
                <li>**Canary Deployment** – Gradually roll out changes to a small set of users before full deployment.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Configuring Rollbacks in Azure DevOps</h2>
        <p>Follow these steps to configure rollback mechanisms:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Enable **versioned artifact storage** to keep previous releases.</li>
            <li>Define a **manual rollback process** for critical releases.</li>
            <li>Use **deployment slots** to test updates before production release.</li>
            <li>Configure automated rollback triggers in Azure Pipelines.</li>
            <li>Monitor deployment logs to identify rollback conditions.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Example YAML for Automated Rollback</h4>
            <pre>
stages:
- stage: Deploy
  displayName: 'Deploy to Production'
  jobs:
  - deployment: DeployWebApp
    environment: 'Production'
    strategy:
      runOnce:
        deploy:
          steps:
          - task: AzureWebApp@1
            inputs:
              azureSubscription: 'AzureServiceConnection'
              appName: 'my-web-app'
              package: '$(Pipeline.Workspace)/drop/*.zip'

          - task: PowerShell@2
            inputs:
              script: |
                if ($LastExitCode -ne 0) {
                    Write-Host "Deployment failed! Rolling back..."
                    az webapp deployment slot swap --resource-group myResourceGroup --name my-web-app --slot staging
                }
            </pre>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/blue-green-deployment.png" alt="Blue-Green Deployment Strategy" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Example of Blue-Green Deployment</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Best Practices for Rollbacks</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Use **deployment slots** for zero-downtime rollbacks.</li>
            <li>Automate rollback conditions to **detect and revert failed deployments**.</li>
            <li>Enable **real-time monitoring** to identify issues quickly.</li>
            <li>Implement **feature flags** to disable faulty features without redeployment.</li>
            <li>Test rollback processes before production deployment.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Rollback strategies in Azure DevOps provide a **safety net** for deployments, ensuring that teams can **quickly recover from failures**. By implementing **automated and manual rollback mechanisms**, organizations can maintain high availability and reduce downtime.
        </p>
        <p>
            The next step is to explore **Infrastructure as Code (IaC) with Azure DevOps** for automated provisioning and management.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="d-flex justify-content-start mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("ApprovalsGates", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">&larr; Previous: Approvals & Gates</a>
    </div>
</div>
